item.text:
{
  "questions": [
    {
      "q": 1,
      "slot_type": "thesis",
      "stem": "Which one of the following best captures the central idea of the passage?",
      "correct": {
        "text": "Debates over heroic eligibility obscure how public praise allocates recognition and shapes expectations about the burdens workers should bear.",
        "why_right": "The passage moves from competing criteria through their institutional use to the commitments praise creates. Its concern shifts from identifying heroes to examining what honour does."
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "Comparing soldiers’ and clinicians’ exposure should establish whether combat involves sufficiently greater danger to reserve the heroic title for soldiers.",
          "mechanism": "prior_belief",
          "why_wrong": "This preserves the anticipated comparison in paragraph 1 after the author displaces it with distinct purposes and public consequences."
        },
        {
          "text": "The competing definitions of heroism deserve reconciliation through a broader standard that includes both historical achievement and exemplary occupational sacrifice.",
          "mechanism": "framework_import",
          "why_wrong": "This imports a classificatory solution where paragraph 4 questions treating public honour as a discoverable property."
        },
        {
          "text": "Public celebrations of healthcare workers express genuine gratitude while allowing institutions to leave dangerous working conditions largely unchanged for staff.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "This captures paragraph 2 but omits the broader shift from eligibility to praise’s role in constituting duties and debts."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 2,
      "slot_type": "structural_function",
      "stem": "The apparent disagreement over the hospital welcome in paragraph 2 is introduced primarily to:",
      "correct": {
        "text": "It provides a seemingly verbal dispute whose public circulation reveals that conferring praise can shape expectations of workers’ endurance.",
        "why_right": "The welcome initially appears to concern taste, but its extension through toys and public rituals exposes institutional consequences. This bridges definitional disagreement and the practical force of honour."
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "It establishes that competing definitions make the dispute a question of wording, with consequences confined to how gratitude is expressed.",
          "mechanism": "framework_import",
          "why_wrong": "This mistakes paragraph 2’s initial appearance of verbal disagreement for its conclusion about expectations and conditions."
        },
        {
          "text": "It illustrates how both competing definitions can explain the same public gesture while preserving their distinct accounts of heroic worth.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "This describes the initial contrast but misses why paragraph 2 follows the welcome into wider institutional circulation."
        },
        {
          "text": "It demonstrates that the egalitarian interpretation better captures institutional intentions because public celebrations chiefly acknowledge courage rather than historical stature.",
          "mechanism": "stance_misread",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 2 examines what praise can do, rather than endorsing egalitarianism as the best account of institutional intentions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 3,
      "slot_type": "closure_reading",
      "stem": "If the passage continued, which one of the following would it most likely discuss next?",
      "correct": {
        "text": "It would examine how institutions’ forms of recognition distribute responsibility for protecting workers and legitimise expectations about continued hazardous service.",
        "why_right": "The ending widens the inquiry from the nurse’s eligibility to commitments made by those welcoming her. Institutional responsibility and expectations develop that opening."
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "It would apply Carlyle’s authoritative historical criterion to establish why healthcare workers remain outside the proper category of publicly recognised heroes.",
          "mechanism": "prior_belief",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 3 recalls Carlyle’s criterion to expose its selective purpose, not to authorise the nurse’s final exclusion."
        },
        {
          "text": "It would develop a common scale of danger through which military achievement and clinical exposure receive proportionate degrees of public honour.",
          "mechanism": "framework_import",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraphs 1 and 4 reject the neutral ranking project that this continuation would restore."
        },
        {
          "text": "It would trace how toys and communal celebrations spread expressions of gratitude from hospital entrances into the wider culture of recognition.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "This extends paragraph 2’s examples while bypassing the ending’s focus on duties, debts, and commitments."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 4,
      "slot_type": "undermine_thesis",
      "stem": "Which one of the following, if established, would most damage the author's central claim?",
      "correct": {
        "text": "Controlled studies show that public heroic titles merely register prior admiration, leaving perceived duties, institutional debts, and acceptable exposure unchanged.",
        "why_right": "This attacks the bridge from praise as description to praise as a practice that helps establish expectations. That constitutive force supports the passage’s displacement of definitional debate."
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "Hospital records show that institutions celebrating healthcare heroes often increased protective equipment budgets and reduced dangerous assignments during the same period.",
          "mechanism": "stance_misread",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 2 claims compatibility with unchanged conditions, not that heroic praise necessarily prevents improvements."
        },
        {
          "text": "Worker surveys show that clinicians receiving heroic praise generally experienced sincere appreciation and regarded public celebrations as valuable recognition of sacrifice.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "Genuine appreciation accords with paragraph 2’s generosity; it does not exclude praise shaping expectations or legitimising exposure."
        },
        {
          "text": "Historical research shows that several clinicians achieved changes in public health whose lasting consequences rivalled those of celebrated military leaders.",
          "mechanism": "framework_import",
          "why_wrong": "Some clinicians meeting historical criteria leaves paragraph 4’s challenge to eligibility as the governing question intact."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 5,
      "slot_type": "contextual_inference",
      "stem": "Based on the passage, each of the following is a reasonable inference about treating public honour as a discoverable property, EXCEPT:",
      "correct": {
        "text": "The principal difficulty lies in finding sufficiently accurate evidence to decide which individuals possess the heroic quality acknowledged by public honours.",
        "why_right": "This converts the author’s objection to the classification framework into a problem of evidential accuracy. Paragraph 4 instead stresses commitments partly created by those conferring honour.",
        "failure_mode": "level"
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "The classification of someone as heroic can obscure commitments that those conferring the title make about that person’s continuing occupational exposure.",
          "mechanism": "passage_supported",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 4 says the nurse’s reception concerns how praise frames duty and that these commitments are made partly by those welcoming her."
        },
        {
          "text": "The rival definitions can appear neutral while selecting different achievements or burdens as the proper objects of collective notice and admiration.",
          "mechanism": "passage_supported",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 3 says choosing between makers of events and bearers of burdens changes who receives notice; paragraph 4 denies neutrality."
        },
        {
          "text": "The search for eligibility can divert attention from whether praise acknowledges an outstanding debt or helps excuse the continuation of exposure.",
          "mechanism": "passage_supported",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 3 raises outstanding debt, and paragraph 4 explicitly contrasts eligibility with acknowledging exposure or excusing its continuation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 6,
      "slot_type": "detail_check",
      "stem": "Which one of the following claims about the competing accounts of heroism is NOT supported by the passage?",
      "correct": {
        "text": "The sacrificial egalitarian treats remuneration as purchasing willingness to accept grave exposure, while retaining admiration for the courage such exposure requires.",
        "why_right": "The egalitarian’s defence explicitly says remuneration is not ordinarily understood to purchase unlimited willingness to die. The option reverses the stated relationship between pay and accepted danger.",
        "failure_mode": "stance"
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "The heroic exceptionalist requires dangerous achievement to have consequences exceeding an occupation’s familiar purposes, rather than treating danger itself as sufficient.",
          "mechanism": "passage_supported",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 1 states that danger alone is insufficient and requires consequences exceeding the familiar purposes of an occupation."
        },
        {
          "text": "The sacrificial egalitarian regards clinicians’ acceptance of grave exposure for strangers as grounds for recognition, despite their work being paid employment.",
          "mechanism": "passage_supported",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 1 gives this exposure as ample warrant and states that employment does not abolish courage."
        },
        {
          "text": "The heroic exceptionalist protects praise against becoming routine occupational approval, while the sacrificial egalitarian resists restricting admiration to historically consequential individuals.",
          "mechanism": "passage_supported",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 1 contrasts protecting praise from a synonym for attendance with protecting ordinary people from historically selective admiration."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 7,
      "slot_type": "stance",
      "stem": "The author's attitude towards the competing definitions of heroism can best be described as:",
      "correct": {
        "text": "The author recognises the moral concern behind each definition but challenges their shared reduction of public honour to individual eligibility.",
        "why_right": "The initial acknowledgement of both positions’ dignity survives the criticism. The author ultimately questions their shared framing rather than simply choosing or dismissing one."
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "The author tentatively favours sacrificial egalitarianism because its broader recognition seems better suited to acknowledging the debts owed to exposed workers.",
          "mechanism": "stance_misread",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 3 also challenges voluntary sacrifice as an account of contracted exposure; paragraph 4 criticises both definitions."
        },
        {
          "text": "The author preserves both definitions as complementary standards whose separate applications can together settle the proper distribution of public heroic honours.",
          "mechanism": "framework_import",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 4 denies that either supplies a neutral instrument; complementary classification does not resolve praise’s practical force."
        },
        {
          "text": "The author values both definitions for distinguishing historical elevation from moral gratitude and clarifying why comparisons of danger remain conceptually inadequate.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "This captures paragraph 1’s distinction but stops before the author challenges the shared treatment of honour in paragraph 4."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": 8,
      "slot_type": "author_vs_reported",
      "stem": "On which one of the following points would the strict heroic exceptionalist and the author most likely disagree?",
      "correct": {
        "text": "A worker’s failure to achieve historically exceptional consequences settles whether public heroic recognition is appropriate.",
        "why_right": "The strict exceptionalist treats exceptional consequence as a gatekeeping criterion. The author questions that criterion’s authority to settle recognition without considering burdens, debts, and what praise does."
      },
      "wrong": [
        {
          "text": "A person’s exposure to occupational danger does not by itself establish achievement of exceptional historical consequence.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "Both can accept this distinction: paragraph 3 explicitly allows that the nurse’s shift need not alter history."
        },
        {
          "text": "A public title distinguishes its recipient from other people whose acknowledged decency receives less ceremonial recognition.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "Paragraph 1 presents ranking as the title’s operation, which the exceptionalist’s desire to reserve the distinction also presupposes."
        },
        {
          "text": "A concern about praise becoming routine occupational approval expresses a defensible reason for scrutinising heroic language.",
          "mechanism": "half_truth",
          "why_wrong": "The author grants this concern dignity in paragraph 1, even while rejecting the exceptionalist’s framework as sufficient."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "length_audit": {
    "correct_longest_count": 0,
    "correct_longest_questions": []
  }
}
